#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Runs one job in one slot of this machine; flow calls it over ssh. Nothing of it stays resident.

usage: executor start --machine NAME      a request (JSON on stdin: slot, project, commit, repo, argv, timeout, clean,
                                          who) becomes a job in its slot; prints {"id", "rundir"} and returns
       executor wait --machine NAME ID    waits for the job's end; prints the machine's alerts while it runs, then
                                          the conclusion as one line "CF-END <json>"
       executor ls --machine NAME         every job of this machine's slots as JSON (newest first)
       executor log --machine NAME ID [-n N]   the last N lines of a job's log
       executor cancel --machine NAME ID  ends a job and prints its conclusion like wait
       executor mirror --project P        the bare mirror of P on this machine (made when missing): flow pushes a
                                          commit GitHub does not have into it
       executor _job RUNDIR               (internal) prepare the work tree on the host, then start the supervisor
       executor _sup RUNDIR               (internal) the supervisor: runs the command, enforces timeout and cancel
       executor _agent RUNDIR             (internal, macOS) runs the supervisor as a launchd daemon and waits for it

A slot runs one job at a time: start takes <slot dir>/.cf/busy (exclusively; one whose job has ended or whose launcher
and supervisor are gone is taken over) and the launcher removes it after the conclusion. A slot that is busy is an
error, not a queue: which job goes where is agreed on before it is started.
On the machine:
  ~/curryflow/mirror/<project>.git   the machine's bare mirror of a project: fetches from GitHub (the project's
                                     "repo" in machines.json) with the host's ssh key, or receives flow's push
  <slot dir>/<project>               the slot's work tree of the project: a clone of the mirror, one checkout per job,
                                     ignored files (.build) stay for incremental builds while the slot dir stays
                                     within SLOT_MAX (cap_slot, before and after each job)
  <slot dir>/.cf/<id>/               a job's run directory: job.json, log, exit.json; removed KEEP after the end
  ~/curryflow/exec/<machine>/alerts.json   what beat found wrong with memory or swapping (wait prints it)
The slot dir is ~/<container> for a slot with an incus container (the container mounts it at the same path) and
~/curryflow/slots/<slot> otherwise. Git runs on the host; the command runs in the slot: through incus exec as the host
user's uid and gid (container), or (macOS) as a launchd daemon of its own. The job's environment has the machine's
deps cache (CF_DEPS_CACHE, mounted read-write into the containers) and the GitHub token of
~/.config/curryflow/github-token (GH_TOKEN, and git rewrites git@github.com: to https with it), so a job fetches its
dependencies itself where it runs.

A job's processes are run by the supervisor, a child subreaper, so every descendant stays below it. The supervisor
ends them (timeout, cancel file, or what is left when the command exits) by walking its own process tree and writes
exit.json last. The launcher creates launch.json itself, exclusively, so a run directory never gets two launchers.

macOS (DARWIN) has no /proc and no child subreaper. The executor asks libproc and Mach what it reads from /proc on
Linux. Each job's supervisor runs as a launchd daemon of its own (cf.<id> in the system domain as this user, started
through sudo and awaited by `executor _agent`, so no login at the Mac is needed): launchd gives a daemon a coalition,
which its descendants cannot leave whatever session, process group or parent they end up with, so the processes of
the job are the processes of that coalition, and the supervisor ends them by it.
"""
